STORY 1 — Scaffold the Spring Boot project · 3 pts
As a developer, I want the existing Maven module converted into a runnable Spring Boot app so I can start building REST endpoints on top of the current domain logic.

Add spring-boot-starter-web, spring-boot-starter-validation, mybatis-spring-boot-starter, postgresql driver to pom.xml
Replace maven-jar-plugin with spring-boot-maven-plugin's repackage goal (keep finalName: team-skeleton so the existing Dockerfile's COPY target/team-skeleton.jar app.jar keeps working unchanged)
Add application.yml with datasource config driven by the same env vars as .env/docker-compose.yml
Decide whether Main.java (currently just prints "Hello world") becomes the @SpringBootApplication entrypoint or is replaced by a new TradeApiApplication
AC: mvn spring-boot:run starts an embedded server and /actuator/health (or equivalent) returns 200; mvn package produces a runnable fat jar.
STORY 2 — Define the OpenAPI contract
As a team, we want the order/account API contract written up front so the Angular (Sprint 9) and Auth (Sprint 8) teams can build against a stable spec. · 3 pts

Author OpenAPI spec covering the 6 endpoints in PDF §9.3: POST /api/v1/orders, DELETE /api/v1/orders/{id}, GET /api/v1/accounts/{id}, /balance, /positions, /orders
Reuse existing shapes: PlaceOrderRequest, OrderResponse, AccountResponse, PositionResponse
AC: Spec reviewed by team; error responses in the spec match the codes in PDF §21 (ACC-404, ACC-403, INS-404, ORD-400, ORD-409, VAL-422).
STORY 3 — MyBatis persistence layer · 8 pts
As a developer, I want Postgres-backed repository implementations so Accounts/Orders/Positions/Instruments persist for real instead of living only in memory.

Implement MyBatis @Mapper interfaces for accounts, orders, positions, instruments (schema in sql/tables/01-04*.sql)
New implementations of the existing AccountRepository, OrderRepository, PositionRepository, InstrumentRepository interfaces, sitting alongside (not replacing) the current InMemory*Repository classes
Remove OrderService's in-memory ordersByIdempotencyKey ConcurrentHashMap and rely on the persisted OrderRepository — the DB already has a UNIQUE constraint on orders.idempotency_key; a duplicate insert should be caught and translated into DuplicateOrderException at the repository/service boundary instead of checked against a non-durable in-memory cache
AC: New repo implementations pass the same behavioural contract as the in-memory versions; data round-trips through real Postgres via docker-compose; placing an order with a reused idempotency key after an app restart still correctly throws DuplicateOrderException (proves the in-memory cache is gone).
STORY 4 — Order controller (place / cancel) · 5 pts
As a trader, I want REST endpoints to place and cancel orders so the frontend can submit trades.

POST /api/v1/orders → wraps OrderService.placeOrder() / OrderProcessor + Buy/Sell strategies
DELETE /api/v1/orders/{id} → cancels a working order; returns 409 ORD-409 if already filled/cancelled
AC: Both endpoints covered by integration tests (MockMvc or similar); idempotency behaviour now backed by Story 3's persisted check, not the removed in-memory map.
STORY 5 — Account/portfolio controller (read endpoints) · 3 pts
As a trader, I want to view account details, balance, positions and order history via REST so the UI has data to render.

GET /api/v1/accounts/{id}, /balance, /positions, /orders
Wraps AccountService/PositionService
AC: All 4 endpoints return correct data for a seeded account; AccountNotFoundException maps to 404.
STORY 6 — Global exception handling · 3 pts
As a developer, I want a @ControllerAdvice that maps the existing exception hierarchy to the PDF's error catalog so clients get consistent error responses.

Create a new ErrorResponse DTO (errorCode, message) in dtos/responses/ — this does not currently exist in the codebase
Map AccountNotFoundException→ACC-404, AccountNotActiveException→ACC-403, InstrumentNotFoundException/InstrumentNotTradableException→INS-404, InsufficientFundsException→ORD-400, InsufficientHoldingsException/DuplicateOrderException→ORD-409, bean-validation failures→VAL-422
AC: Each exception type returns the correct HTTP status and an ErrorResponse body matching PDF §21.1.
STORY 7 — Request validation & structured logging · 2 pts
As an ops engineer, I want validated requests and structured logs so bad input is rejected cleanly and issues are traceable.

Confirm @Valid/Bean Validation fires through the controller layer (validation annotations already exist on DTOs)
Add structured logging (SLF4J + JSON encoder) on order placement, cancellation, and error paths
AC: Invalid payload → 422 VAL-422; logs show request correlation for a placed order.
STORY 8 — Verify the Dockerised service end-to-end · 1 pt
As a team, we want the existing Docker setup confirmed working now that the jar is Spring Boot–packaged.

No new Dockerfile/compose changes expected — Dockerfile and the app service in docker-compose.yml (mapping 8081:8080) already match a Spring Boot fat jar; this story just proves it end-to-end once Story 1's repackage change lands
AC: docker-compose up -d produces a working API reachable on 8081, backed by the postgres container.
STORY 9 — Integration tests & showcase readiness · 5 pts
As a team, we want end-to-end confidence before the Sprint 6 showcase.

Integration tests covering the happy path + each business rule in PDF §20 (funds check, holdings check, idempotency, inactive account, untradable instrument)
Update README.md with API run/test instructions
AC: Full order lifecycle (place → balance/position updated → cancel) demonstrable via curl/Postman against the Dockerised service; matches PDF §9.6 showcase checklist (running service, API demo, Postgres persistence, container, feature branch merged to develop).



